-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/1a2ee204-dd9b-465a-b37d-ac8c7d5379df
-- title:
--   $\pi_1(\mathbb R^2\setminus(0,0))\cong\mathbb Z$, generated by the anticlockwise unit loop
-- statement:
--   The fundamental group of the punctured plane is the integers, and the isomorphism counts anticlockwise windings around the origin. Precisely, with base point $p_0=(0,-1)$ there is a group isomorphism
--
--   $$
--   e:\ \pi_1\bigl(\mathbb R^2\setminus(0,0),\,p_0\bigr)\ \xrightarrow{\ \cong\ }\ \mathbb Z
--   \qquad\text{with}\qquad e\bigl([\sigma]\bigr)=1,
--   $$
--
--   where $\sigma(t)=(\sin 2\pi t,-\cos 2\pi t)$ is the loop that goes once anticlockwise around the unit circle. Such an $e$ is the winding number.
--
--   The paper cites this fact from Hatcher's *Algebraic Topology*. The proof of Theorem 3 uses it to know that a loop homotopic to $\sigma$ is not null-homotopic in the punctured plane.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The punctured plane is `{p : ℝ × ℝ // p ≠ 0}`; $\pi_1$ is Mathlib's `FundamentalGroup`; $\mathbb Z$ is `Multiplicative ℤ`, so that the group operation is addition of integers.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: two loops are equivalent if there is a continuous T : [0,1]² → X with T(0,·) = γ₁, T(1,·) = γ₂ (p.13, no endpoint condition) → Path.Homotopic: homotopy rel endpoints (fixed base point at every stage); reason: read literally, any two loops in a path-connected space are equivalent (T(s,t) = γ₁((1−s)t) contracts γ₁ to a point, then reverse for γ₂), which would make π₁(ℝ² ∖ 0) trivial against the paper's own claim that it is ℤ (p.13); the based homotopy is the standard definition of π₁ in the cited reference [17] and the one the proofs use
--   - encoding: π₁(ℝ² ∖ (0,0)) with no base point named; "identified with the number of times it winds anticlockwise" → FundamentalGroup at (0, −1) ≃* Multiplicative ℤ sending the class of t ↦ (sin 2πt, −cos 2πt) to ofAdd 1; reason: the space is path connected, so the base point does not matter (p.13); an isomorphism onto ℤ sending the once-anticlockwise loop to 1 is unique and is the anticlockwise winding count
-- source:
--   Ramachandran, Shu & Wang, Hidden convexity, optimization, and algorithms on rotation matrices, arXiv:2304.08596v2, §4.1, p.13 ("the fundamental group of the punctured plane R2 \ (0, 0) is Z. Explicitly, any loop in R2 \ (0, 0) can be identified with the number of times it winds anticlockwise around the origin"), cited from Hatcher [17]

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HiddenConvexSO_puncturedBase
import Definitions.Def_HiddenConvexSO_standardLoop

namespace HiddenConvexSO

/-- **π₁ of the punctured plane is `ℤ`, counted by anticlockwise winding.**
Ramachandran, Shu & Wang, arXiv:2304.08596v2, §4.1, p.13 (PDF p.13), cited from Hatcher [17]:
"the fundamental group of the punctured plane R2 \ (0, 0) is Z. Explicitly, any loop in R2 \ (0, 0)
can be identified with the number of times it winds anticlockwise around the origin."

There is a group isomorphism from `π₁(ℝ² ∖ (0,0), (0,−1))` onto `ℤ` (written multiplicatively) that
sends the class of the loop `t ↦ (sin 2πt, −cos 2πt)`, which winds once anticlockwise, to `1`.

**Formalization Note.** The punctured plane is `{p : ℝ × ℝ // p ≠ 0}`; the base point is `(0, −1)`,
where the standard loop starts (the paper fixes none; p.13 notes independence of the base point).
`π₁` is Mathlib's `FundamentalGroup` (classes of loops up to homotopy rel endpoints), and `ℤ` is
`Multiplicative ℤ` so that the group law is addition. The generator clause is the formal content of
"identified with the number of times it winds anticlockwise". -/
theorem fundamentalGroup_puncturedPlane :
    ∃ e : FundamentalGroup {p : ℝ × ℝ // p ≠ 0} puncturedBase ≃* Multiplicative ℤ,
      e (FundamentalGroup.fromPath ⟦standardLoop⟧) = Multiplicative.ofAdd 1 := by sorry

end HiddenConvexSO
